/* ============================================================================
geometric-sum.epher -- sum of the first n terms of a geometric sequence

The sum of the first n terms of a geometric sequence is
S_n = a (1 - r^n) / (1 - r), since the pairing trick subtracts the tail
a r^n from the shifted sum (any algebra text, e.g. Stewart, Redlin and
Watson, "College Algebra", 7th ed., sec. 9.2). With a = 3, r = 2 the first
10 terms sum to 3 (2^10 - 1) = 3069, cross-checked by adding the terms
one by one in a quiet while loop. All values are exact.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/algebra/sequences-series/geometric-sum.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\algebra\sequences-series\geometric-sum.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/algebra/sequences-series/geometric-sum.epher

The knobs to edit are a, r and n below.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs ---------------------------------------------------------------
const a = 3                      // first term
const r = 2                      // common ratio
const n = 10                     // number of terms to add

// ---- helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------
def gn(a, r, n) = a * r ^ (n - 1)                  // the n-th term
def gs(a, r, n) = a * (1 - r ^ n) / (1 - r)        // sum of the first n terms

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("S10 by formula:", gs(a, r, n))
// add the terms one by one (a for loop would echo all 10 partial sums)
acc = {0, 0}
while acc[1] < n do acc = {acc[1] + 1, acc[2] + gn(a, r, acc[1] + 1)}
print("S10 by adding:", acc[2])
print("formula equals the sum:", gs(a, r, n) == acc[2])
/* ---- expected output ----
= 3
= 2
= 10
= S10 by formula: 3069
= {0, 0}
= S10 by adding: 3069
= formula equals the sum: true
*/
